  "body": "### Summary\n\nAs a reader, when a member posts a rehosted image in its **medium** size (a lighter rendition, so the page stays light), I want the viewer to open the **full-size** image, so that a screenshot posted that way is readable on a phone. Today it stays an unreadable « pixel soup ». (Operator feedback, 2026-11-15, from the HFR+ topic: members asked for it on the evening of 2026-11-15, and the maintainer confirmed the legacy app was meant to do it on img3 and a few other hosts, but it does not work there either.)\n\n### Observed\n\n- A member posts an `img3.super-h.fr` image as the **medium** rendition: the file name carries `.md.` (`…/images/<yyyy>/<mm>/<dd>/<name>.md.jpg`).\n- In the reader the picture is small, which is intended. Opening it in the viewer still shows the medium file, so the text in the screenshot cannot be read.\n- Members say that removing `.md` from the URL gives the full image. Legacy has the same gap, though the maintainer says it was meant to work.\n\n### Today (dev-swift)\n\n- `ImageHostTable.fullSizeURL` (`Packages/HFRKit/Sources/HFRNetwork/ImageHosts/ImageHostTable.swift:64`) upgrades a thumbnail to the full image in the viewer (LOT-056, LOT-109). For `img3.super-h.fr` the only rule removes `.th.` (`ImageHostTable.swift:20`, `.fileNameMarker(pathPrefix: \"/images/\", marker: \".th.\")`).\n- There is **no rule for the medium marker `.md.`**, so the viewer opens the medium file.\n\n### Sources for the `.md.` shape (no guessing, AGENTS.md §3.1)\n\n- Legacy upload history stores `mediumURL` as `<name>.md.jpg` next to `<name>.th.jpg` and `<name>.jpg`:\n  - `tooling/legacy-oracle/samples/legacy-stores/upload-history-after-corrupt-rewrite.json`;\n  - `Packages/HFRKit/Tests/HFRStorageTests/LegacyImport/LegacyFormatsTests.swift:75`.\n- The upload answer has an `image.medium` rendition (HFR-PROTOCOL §LOT-056, `U01`/`U02`). It was captured only for a 16×16 image, where medium is `null`.\n- **To capture (Mac)**, before the rule is called verified:\n  - the upload answer of a large image, showing `image.medium.url`;\n  - one GET of a `.md.` URL and of its full-size counterpart, with their `Referer` behaviour.\n\n  If no capture exists, open a capture issue naming this one.\n\n### Requested\n\n1. Add the medium marker to the `img3.super-h.fr` rule: `.md.` is removed like `.th.`. The viewer then opens `<name>.jpg`, and the reader keeps showing the medium in the post, as the poster intended.\n2. Check the other hosts in the table for a medium rendition, from legacy code or captures only. Add a rule where it is sourced, and list the others as not sourced.\n3. If the full-size file fails to load (404, refused), the viewer falls back to the medium.\n4. Related: #912 (a picture inside a link opens the linked full-size image). Both go through `fullSizeURL` and policy C; keep one implementation.\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] An `img3.super-h.fr` `.md.` image opens full-size in the viewer; a `.th.` one still does; any other URL is unchanged.\n- [ ] A failing full-size fetch falls back to the medium.\n- [ ] `ImageHostTableTests` gains the `.md.` cases and is shown to fail without the rule.\n- [ ] HFR-PROTOCOL §LOT-056 records the medium shape and its source, labelled « code » until captured and « verified » after.\n- [ ] Ledger: a `changelog` entry, since this fixes a legacy gap (« aperçu médium ouvert en pleine taille dans la visionneuse »).\n\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
